# Code owners: who is asked to review what.
#
# Fill this in with real GitHub handles before the first external pull request. An
# empty CODEOWNERS file is the normal starting state, and a review request to a
# handle that does not exist is silently dropped rather than failing loudly, which
# is the worse direction — so say so here instead of inventing owners.
#
# The roles correspond to MAINTAINERS.md, and the reason they are split by path is
# that the two kinds of judgement this project needs are not the same kind: a
# change to the protocol, the evidence ladder or a documented claim has to be read
# by somebody who will answer for the claim, and a change to a grammar table does
# not need that, but does need the adapter's tests to pass.
#
# Example, once the handles exist:
#
#   *                                       @lead-maintainer
#   /crates/swp-core/                       @protocol-owners
#   /crates/swp-crypto/                     @protocol-owners
#   /crates/swp-adapters/                   @adapter-owners
#   /docs/                                  @docs-owners
#   /crates/swp-test-suite/tests/docs/      @docs-owners
#   /CLA.md                                 @legal-review
#   /CLA/signatures/                        @owner
#   /.github/workflows/                     @release-owners
#
# `CLA/signatures/` holds signed statements, which are records rather than code:
# they change by an owner's hand only, because the person asserting a grant cannot
# be the person accepting it.
